🚨 **BLOCKING: a fail-closed (empty machineAuthor) ledger read silently strips the park label from genuinely parked bugs**

`bugBookkeeping.loadBugBookkeeping` fails closed: with no configured machine-author allowlist it degrades to `tcMaintenanceLedger.emptyLedger()` (bugBookkeeping.js:57–61, "no configured machine identity trusts nothing"). In this dispatcher that empty ledger means `differentialRequests(...)` returns `[]`, and this branch then **removes `tc_differential_pending` from the bug** and returns `nothing_parked`:

```js
var requests = tcMaintenanceLedger.differentialRequests(vars.ledger);
if (!requests.length) {
    removeLabelQuietly(tracker, ticketKey, parkLabel);
    return { success: true, action: 'nothing_parked', ticketKey: ticketKey };
}
```

The bug may carry a genuinely parked request that the fail-closed parse refused to read. After the label is stripped:

- the dispatcher rule's JQL (`labels = tc_differential_pending`) no longer matches → the request is orphaned forever;
- the generator rule's new skip on `tc_differential_pending` (sm.json:200) no longer applies → a fresh TC generation dispatch fires on a bug whose differential request was never resolved — exactly the unattributed re-dispatch this PR exists to prevent.

No marker is posted (`ledgerChanged` is false), no comment is posted — the park label vanishes silently. This contradicts the PR's own contract ("the dispatcher never bounces on unavailable evidence … stays parked fail-safe") and the deploy note you added to `docs/machine-factory-integration.md` explicitly anticipates deployments running TC triage with an empty allowlist (the guard warning exists for that case — but `triageAllowlistWarning` is wired only into `postStoryTestAutomationResults`, not here).

Fix: at `action` startup, resolve the allowlist (reuse `bugBookkeeping.triageAllowlistWarning(params, config)`); when it resolves empty, log the warning and bail out **without** clearing the park label (e.g. `action: 'unconfigured_parked'`), leaving the stall digest-visible. Optionally distinguish "empty ledger because fail-closed" from "no findings" via the authorLogins you already pass to the loader.

⚠️ **IMPORTANT: ledger-controlled strings are interpolated into shell commands without validation**

The dispatcher's verdict inputs travel from the TC maintenance ledger marker (a ticket comment) straight into shell commands:

- `buildRunCommand` (js/tcDifferentialDispatcher.js:98) substitutes `{testPath}` — the ledger finding's `testPath`, harvested from test-run output in the target repo — into `tcRunCommand`, then `runDifferential` executes it via `cli_execute_command`. A `testPath` containing `;`, `&&`, `$(...)`, backticks, or an option-like leading `-` changes what the factory shell runs.
- `runDifferential` interpolates `request.lastGreenSha` unvalidated: `git -C <dir> checkout <sha>` (line 142) and `git -C <dir> fetch origin <sha>` (line 153). A non-SHA value with shell metacharacters injects commands; a value starting with `-` is argument injection into git.

Mitigations that already exist: the ledger parse is fail-closed against the machineAuthor allowlist, so only machine-authored markers are read. But those markers embed strings produced by the target repo's own test output/file paths — a repo that accepts external contributions — and `cli_execute_command` runs in the factory's shell context, which is broader than the repo checkout. Defense-in-depth is cheap here, and the codebase already has the perfect landing spot: the `unavailable` route.

Suggested validation before building any command:

```js
var SHA_RE = /^[0-9a-f]{7,64}$/i;
function safeSha(sha) { return SHA_RE.test(String(sha || '').trim()) ? String(sha).trim() : null; }
function safeTestPath(p) {
    var s = String(p || '').trim();
    if (!s || /[\s;&|<>$`\\\"'\n]/.test(s) || s.indexOf('..') !== -1) return null;
    return s;
}
```

A failed check routes to the existing `{ outcome: 'unavailable', detail: '...' }` fail-safe (stays parked, digest-visible) instead of executing anything.

💡 **SUGGESTION: unused `bugKey` parameter in `roleFieldsFromDescription`**

```js
function roleFieldsFromDescription(description, bugKey) {
    var marker = parseRoleMarker(description);
    if (!marker) return {};
    if (marker.kind === 'verifies') {
        return { role: 'verifies', verifies: marker.key };
    }
    return { role: 'regression guard', regressionGuard: true };
}
```

`bugKey` is never used, yet the signature suggests the reader performs sibling detection (`Verifies: <other-bug>` → sibling) at this layer. That resolution actually happens later in `tcTriage.resolveTcRole` — which is fine — but the parameter misleads callers into thinking the annotation is bug-scoped. Either drop the parameter, or use it to also return e.g. `isSibling` so the automation leg's input carries the resolved relationship. `annotateWithRoles` (its only caller) passes `storyKey` today, so dropping it is a one-line cleanup plus test touch-ups.

💡 **SUGGESTION: `summaryOf` / `ticketFields` — duplicated field accessor**

```js
function summaryOf(ticketDetails) {
    var fields = (ticketDetails && ticketFields(ticketDetails)) || {};
    return fields.summary || null;
}

function ticketFields(ticketDetails) {
    return (ticketDetails && ticketDetails.fields) || {};
}
```

`ticketFields(ticketDetails)` already normalizes null/undefined to `{}`, so the outer `(... && ...) || {}` in `summaryOf` re-does the same guard twice. One accessor suffices:

```js
function summaryOf(ticketDetails) {
    return ticketFields(ticketDetails).summary || null;
}
```

Same file, `issueTypeName` keeps its own `(ticketDetails && ticketDetails.fields) || {}` — a third copy of the same normalization. Consolidating on `ticketFields` everywhere removes the duplication.

💡 **SUGGESTION: say it in the report when the per-tick dispatch cap truncates requests**

`dispatchRequests` processes only `requests.slice(0, MAX_DISPATCHES_PER_TICK)` (10 per tick); anything beyond stays parked for a later tick — reasonable pacing. But the route report posted to the ticket is built purely from the routed subsets, so a bug with 12 parked requests shows a report about 10 with no hint that 2 were deferred this tick (they remain visible only in the ledger digest).

One line fixes the observability gap:

```js
if (requests.length > bounded.length) {
    sections.push(MAX_DISPATCHES_PER_TICK + '-request-per-tick cap: ' +
        (requests.length - bounded.length) + ' parked request(s) deferred to the next tick.');
}
```

(pass `requests.length` into `buildReportComment`, or return the truncated count from `dispatchRequests`).

## Trigger label lifecycle fixed — the job now lifts its own lock

`sm_tc_differential_triggered` is removed by the dispatcher itself, following the `quotaParkProbe.liftPark` doctrine (the job manages its trigger label's full lifecycle — the SM-side `addLabel` stays as the in-flight crash guard):

- `js/tcDifferentialDispatcher.js` — new `clearDispatchLabels()` removes **both** the park label and `sm_tc_differential_triggered` (`TRIGGER_LABEL_DEFAULT`) when nothing remains parked: on the `nothing_parked` early return and after the dispatch tail. Requests still parked after a tick (cap-deferred or stalled) keep **both** labels, so the next tick re-runs and the quiet-bump path (`occurrences > 1`) is now reachable in production.
- This also un-deads a consumed bug: after the labels are lifted, a later park on the same bug dispatches again (the generator rule's `tc_differential_pending` skip no longer strands it), and a crash mid-dispatch no longer locks forever — the next tick retries.

Tests (`js/unit-tests/test_tcDifferentialDispatcher.js`, "trigger label lifecycle" suite):

- a fully consumed dispatch removes both labels;
- `nothing_parked` also clears the trigger label;
- a **second park on the same bug dispatches again** (round-trip test — fresh ledger marker, fresh scripted run);
- cap-deferred requests keep both labels for the retry.

## Ledger-controlled strings are validated before any command is built

Defense-in-depth added exactly along the suggested shape — a failed check routes to the existing `unavailable` fail-safe (stays parked, digest-visible), nothing is executed:

- `js/tcDifferentialDispatcher.js` — `safeSha()` (7–64 hex chars, `^[0-9a-f]{7,64}$/i`) and `safeTestPath()` (rejects empty, option-like leading `-`, `..` escapes, and whitespace/shell metacharacters `; & | < > $ \` \ " '` newline). `runDifferential()` validates **first** — before `git fetch origin <sha>`, before `git checkout <sha>`, before the `tcRunCommand` substitution — so a hostile `lastGreenSha` or `testPath` harvested from a target repo's test output can no longer inject commands or git options. The rejection detail is generic (no echo of the rejected value) and travels through the `unavailable` route: one explanation comment, then quiet ledger bumps.
- Exported for direct unit testing alongside `runDifferential`.

Tests (`js/unit-tests/test_tcDifferentialDispatcher.js`, "sha/testPath validation" suite):

- `main; rm -rf /` as `lastGreenSha` → `stalled`, the string never reaches the shell;
- leading-dash `-rf` → no checkout attempted (git argument injection blocked);
- `tests/x.py; curl evil.sh` as `testPath` → no `pytest`, no injected command;
- `../secrets/test.py` and `-v` → unavailable, requests stay parked with the occurrence bump;
- `safeSha`/`safeTestPath` accept exactly the legitimate shapes (hex length boundaries 7/64, clean repo-relative paths).
- All existing fixtures moved to realistic hex SHAs (`cafed00dbee` / `facade12345`) — the fake non-SHA markers would now (correctly) be rejected.
